\title{A derivative-based regex engine for Reussir}
\taxon{Design}
\date{2026-11-16}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{schrodingerzy}
\tag{design}
\tag{stdlib}
\tag{regex}
\meta{status}{draft}
\meta{created}{2026-11-16}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} draft, for discussion. The sets of bytes and of characters and the classes of bytes (\pr{1039}), the UTF-8 sequences of a set of characters (\pr{1041}), the regex tree with its translation to bytes (\pr{1042}) and the nodes with their derivatives (\pr{1043}) are in the standard library; the automaton with its table and runs is in review as \pr{1044}; nothing else is implemented.}

\p{A regex engine written in Reussir whose automaton is built from Brzozowski derivatives when the program runs. Its design follows Paguroidea, a lexer generator that builds its automaton when the program is compiled, and replaces each compile-time device of Paguroidea by a run-time one.}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-scope]{
\title{What the first version covers}

\ul{
\li{A regex is built with a tree builder. Parsing a pattern string is left to a later version.}
\li{The matcher reads bytes and nothing else. A leaf of a regex is a set of bytes or a set of characters; a set of characters is turned into UTF-8 byte sequences when the rules become a matcher, so UTF-8 text is matched without being decoded.}
\li{A matcher holds a list of rules, each a regex with an action. A match names the rule that wins and every other rule that matches the same text. This is the lexer form of Paguroidea: longest match, the earliest rule on a tie, anchored at the current position.}
\li{Input may arrive in pieces.}
\li{A regex may use intersection and complement, as in Paguroidea.}
\li{Transitions are held in flat tables, built completely when the rules are turned into a matcher. A state that loops on itself has a run that is skipped with vector comparisons or through a lookup table that all states share, as in Paguroidea.}
}

\p{Left to later versions: a pattern parser, search for a match anywhere in the input, captures, lookaround, counted repetition beyond what the builder expands, Unicode property tables and case folding.}
}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-example]{
\title{A worked example}

\p{Three rules over bytes:}

\pre{\verb~|
ident  = [a-z]+        action: Token::Ident
number = [0-9]+        action: Token::Number
space  = ' '+          action: Token::Space
~}

\p{\strong{Classes.} The sets the rules mention are \code{\verb~|[a-z]~}, \code{\verb~|[0-9]~} and \code{\verb~|' '~}. They split the 256 bytes into four classes that no rule can tell apart: \code{\verb~|0 = [a-z]~}, \code{\verb~|1 = [0-9]~}, \code{\verb~|2 = ' '~}, \code{\verb~|3 = everything else~}. A table of 256 entries maps a byte to its class.}

\p{\strong{States.} A state is one regex per rule. The start state is \code{\verb~|([a-z]+, [0-9]+, ' '+)~}. The derivative of a regex \code{\verb~|r~} by a class is a regex for what may follow one byte of that class. Deriving the start state by class 0 gives \code{\verb~|([a-z]*, ∅, ∅)~}, where \code{\verb~|∅~} matches nothing; its first component accepts the empty string, so the state accepts with the rule \code{\verb~|ident~}. Deriving that state by class 0 again gives the same state.}

\table{
\tr{\th{State}\th{regexes}\th{accepts}\th{class 0}\th{class 1}\th{class 2}\th{class 3}}
\tr{\td{0}\td{\code{\verb~|([a-z]+, [0-9]+, ' '+)~}}\td{}\td{1}\td{2}\td{3}\td{dead}}
\tr{\td{1}\td{\code{\verb~|([a-z]*, ∅, ∅)~}}\td{ident}\td{1}\td{dead}\td{dead}\td{dead}}
\tr{\td{2}\td{\code{\verb~|(∅, [0-9]*, ∅)~}}\td{number}\td{dead}\td{2}\td{dead}\td{dead}}
\tr{\td{3}\td{\code{\verb~|(∅, ∅, ' '*)~}}\td{space}\td{dead}\td{dead}\td{3}\td{dead}}
}

\p{\strong{Matching \code{\verb~|abc 42~}.} State 0, byte \code{\verb~|a~} is class 0, go to state 1, which accepts \code{\verb~|ident~}: remember "ident, length 1". \code{\verb~|b~}, \code{\verb~|c~} stay in state 1: "ident, length 3". The space leads to the dead state, so the answer is the last one remembered: the action of \code{\verb~|ident~}, 3 bytes.}

\p{\strong{Acceleration.} State 1 loops on itself for class 0 and leaves on every other byte. Its loop is one range, so the matcher can test 16 or more bytes at once with \code{\verb~|(b >= 'a') & (b <= 'z')~} on a vector and take \code{\verb~|first_set~} of the complement to find where the run ends. This is what Paguroidea emits as code for such a state; here the two bounds are data stored with the state.}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-example-characters]{
\title{The same with a set of characters}

\p{One rule, \code{\verb~|ident = S+~}, where \code{\verb~|S~} is the set of characters \code{\verb~|[a-zα-ω]~}. The letters α to ω are U+03B1 to U+03C9, two bytes each in UTF-8, so the set becomes this regex over bytes when the matcher is built (decision 3):}

\pre{\verb~|
S  =  [61-7A]  |  CE [B1-BF]  |  CF [80-89]
~}

\p{The byte sets in it are \code{\verb~|[61-7A]~}, \code{\verb~|CE~}, \code{\verb~|CF~}, \code{\verb~|[80-89]~} and \code{\verb~|[B1-BF]~}. With every other byte they make six classes.}

\table{
\tr{\th{State}\th{regex}\th{accepts}\th{\code{\verb~|[61-7A]~}}\th{\code{\verb~|CE~}}\th{\code{\verb~|CF~}}\th{\code{\verb~|[80-89]~}}\th{\code{\verb~|[B1-BF]~}}\th{other}}
\tr{\td{0}\td{\code{\verb~|S+~}}\td{}\td{1}\td{2}\td{3}\td{dead}\td{dead}\td{dead}}
\tr{\td{1}\td{\code{\verb~|S*~}}\td{ident}\td{1}\td{2}\td{3}\td{dead}\td{dead}\td{dead}}
\tr{\td{2}\td{\code{\verb~|[B1-BF] S*~}}\td{}\td{dead}\td{dead}\td{dead}\td{dead}\td{1}\td{dead}}
\tr{\td{3}\td{\code{\verb~|[80-89] S*~}}\td{}\td{dead}\td{dead}\td{dead}\td{1}\td{dead}\td{dead}}
}

\p{\strong{Matching \code{\verb~|aβ!~}}, the bytes \code{\verb~|61 CE B2 21~}. \code{\verb~|61~} leads to state 1: "ident, length 1". \code{\verb~|CE~} leads to state 2, which does not accept: the matcher is in the middle of a character. \code{\verb~|B2~} leads back to state 1: "ident, length 3". \code{\verb~|21~} leads to the dead state, and the answer is 3 bytes.}

\ul{
\li{If the input, or one piece of it, ends after \code{\verb~|CE~}, the scan waits in state 2. Nothing else is needed for a character cut in two.}
\li{If the byte after \code{\verb~|CE~} is not in \code{\verb~|[B1-BF]~}, because the text is malformed or the character is another one such as \code{\verb~|ά~} (\code{\verb~|CE AC~}), state 2 leads to the dead state and the match stays at length 1.}
\li{States 0 and 1 have equal rows and share one row of the table.}
}
}
}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-decisions]{
\title{Design decisions}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-normalization]{
\title{1. Regexes are normalized when they are constructed}

\p{Paguroidea derives a plain tree and normalizes it in a separate pass, and compares states as whole trees. The engines that build automata at run time (the .NET non-backtracking engine, RE\verb~|#~, derivre, the LLVM libc matcher) all do the opposite: every regex node is made by a constructor that applies the rewrite rules and looks the result up in a table of existing nodes, so equal regexes are the same node.}

\p{Chosen: constructors with a node table. A node is a small integer.}

\ul{
\li{Equality and hashing of a state are operations on integers, not on trees.}
\li{Each node stores whether it accepts the empty string, computed once.}
\li{The rules are those Paguroidea applies in its pass: union and intersection as sorted lists without duplicates (which makes them associative, commutative and idempotent), the unit and zero laws, \code{\verb~|(r*)* = r*~}, \code{\verb~|¬¬r = r~}, and merging of the sets inside one union or intersection.}
}

\p{The tree the caller builds is separate from these nodes: it is an ordinary value with no table behind it, so building a regex needs no state. Compiling a list of rules turns the trees into nodes.}

\p{Not in the first version: the further rules of RE\verb~|#~ (factoring \code{\verb~|ab|ac~} into \code{\verb~|a(b|c)~}, absorption, merging of counted loops) and the subsumption tests of .NET. They reduce the number of states for some patterns and can be added to the constructors later without changing anything else.}
}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-bytes]{
\title{2. The matcher reads bytes only}

\p{Text is UTF-8: a character takes one to four bytes. A regex over characters can meet such text in three ways.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Way}\th{What the loop steps on}\th{States}\th{Per character}}
\tr{\td{decode in the loop}\td{characters}\td{one step per character}\td{a decode, and a search in a map of code points beyond ASCII}}
\tr{\td{translate the regex to bytes when it is built}\td{bytes}\td{more, for sets beyond ASCII}\td{one table load per byte}}
\tr{\td{decode the text first}\td{\code{\verb~|char~} units of four bytes}\td{one step per character}\td{the text is copied at four bytes per character}}
}

\p{Chosen: translate, as the automata of Rust's \code{\verb~|regex-automata~} do. There is one alphabet, the 256 bytes.}

\ul{
\li{The matching loop has one case: load a byte, look up its class, look up the next state. It has no decoding, no character that is incomplete, and no malformed character; decision 4 says what those situations become.}
\li{There are never more than 256 classes, whatever sets the rules use, because classes of bytes are disjoint and none is empty. A set of classes is 256 bits and nothing can overflow it.}
\li{A piece of input may end anywhere, also inside a character.}
\li{A skip works on bytes, where vectors look ahead (decision 7).}
}

\p{The price is more states for sets beyond ASCII; decision 3 gives numbers.}

\p{\strong{Sets of bytes.} A set of bytes is 256 bits, \code{\verb~|Bits~}, held as four 64-bit words in one vector \code{\verb~|Simd<[u64; 4]>~}. Two other representations were measured against four separate \code{\verb~|u64~} fields: two \code{\verb~|u128~} fields, and the vector. Instructions of each function compiled alone, for x86-64 without extensions and for \code{\verb~|x86-64-v3~}:}

\table{
\tr{\th{Function}\th{4 × u64}\th{2 × u128}\th{Simd}\th{4 × u64, v3}\th{2 × u128, v3}\th{Simd, v3}}
\tr{\td{union, intersection}\td{10}\td{10}\td{3}\td{10}\td{10}\td{2}}
\tr{\td{difference}\td{17}\td{17}\td{5}\td{13}\td{15}\td{2}}
\tr{\td{is empty}\td{5}\td{5}\td{7}\td{5}\td{5}\td{4}}
\tr{\td{number of members}\td{68}\td{68}\td{36}\td{9}\td{10}\td{17}}
\tr{\td{smallest member}\td{19}\td{17}\td{24}\td{16}\td{16}\td{24}}
\tr{\td{range}\td{67}\td{65}\td{148}\td{62}\td{61}\td{30}}
\tr{\td{one member}\td{66}\td{63}\td{18}\td{61}\td{59}\td{11}}
\tr{\td{membership}\td{72}\td{73}\td{13}\td{68}\td{69}\td{18}}
\tr{\td{equality}\td{11}\td{16}\td{7}\td{11}\td{15}\td{5}}
}

\p{Time against the four \code{\verb~|u64~} fields, median of 7 runs:}

\table{
\tr{\th{Workload}\th{2 × u128}\th{Simd}\th{2 × u128, v3}\th{Simd, v3}}
\tr{\td{ranges and every operation mixed}\td{0.91}\td{0.71}\td{0.79}\td{0.26}}
\tr{\td{ranges}\td{0.87}\td{0.65}\td{0.81}\td{0.16}}
\tr{\td{membership of random numbers}\td{0.40}\td{0.12}\td{0.37}\td{0.10}}
\tr{\td{sets built one member at a time}\td{0.78}\td{0.17}\td{0.76}\td{0.14}}
\tr{\td{equality}\td{0.90}\td{0.59}\td{0.94}\td{0.58}}
\tr{\td{classes of bytes: refine with 24 ranges, then inside}\td{0.99}\td{1.06}\td{1.01}\td{1.05}}
}

\p{A \code{\verb~|u128~} is two 64-bit registers, so the bitwise functions are the same instructions. The vector is two to ten times faster wherever a set is built or tested, and 5 to 6 percent slower in the classes of bytes, where the smallest member is asked for often.}

\p{\strong{Classes of bytes.} Two bytes are in one class when every set of bytes in the rules has both or neither. The classes are computed once for all rules of a matcher, as .NET and RE\verb~|#~ do; Paguroidea computes classes per state, which suits generated code but not one table indexed by state and class. With one alphabet there is no trait: the classes are one type with three functions.}

\pre{\verb~|
ByteClasses::whole()        one class of all bytes
classes.refine(set)         every class split into its part inside `set` and its part outside
classes.inside(set)         the numbers of the classes that make up `set`, as a `Bits`
~}

\p{The classes of a matcher are a fold of \code{\verb~|refine~} over every set of bytes in the translated rules, starting from \code{\verb~|whole()~}. They are held as a list of disjoint \code{\verb~|Bits~} in the order of their smallest members, and the number of a class is its place in that list. \code{\verb~|refine~} cannot fail and \code{\verb~|inside~} cannot overflow.}
}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-utf8]{
\title{3. A set of characters becomes UTF-8 byte sequences when rules become a matcher}

\p{The builder takes a set of characters as a \code{\verb~|CharSet~}. The tree keeps it as a set until the rules are turned into a matcher. Until then set algebra is done on code points, where it is exact and cheap: the sets inside one union or intersection are merged (decision 1), and the complement of a set is taken there (decision 4).}

\p{\strong{How a set of characters is held.} A set gives every code point one of two values, in or out, and the value changes at only a few code points. It is held as those code points, in the ordered map \code{\verb~|WavlMap<u32, bool>~} of \code{\verb~|std::collections::pure~}: the entry \code{\verb~|(k, v)~} says the value is \code{\verb~|v~} from \code{\verb~|k~} up to the next key. The value from zero is a field beside the map, so a lookup always has an answer and needs no panic. ICU's \code{\verb~|UnicodeSet~} holds a set the same way, in a sorted array.}

\pre{\verb~|
[a-zα-ω]      from 0: out,   61 → in,   7B → out,   3B1 → in,   3CA → out
~}

\table{
\tr{\th{Operation}\th{How}\th{Cost}}
\tr{\td{is a character in a set}\td{the entry at or below its code point}\td{a logarithm of the number of changes}}
\tr{\td{give a range one value}\td{remove the keys inside it, insert its two ends, drop an end where the value does not change}\td{a logarithm, and the keys removed}}
\tr{\td{union, intersection, difference}\td{walk the set with fewer changes and give its ranges a value in the other}\td{the smaller set times a logarithm}}
\tr{\td{the stretches of members in order}\td{one walk of the map}\td{linear}}
}

\p{Surrogates, U+D800 to U+DFFF, and numbers above U+10FFFF are in no set.}

\p{\code{\verb~|IntMap~}, the Patricia map of the same library, has every operation used here. Both were measured under the same code for sets, in one program, release profile, whole process, median of 11 runs in milliseconds:}

\table{
\tr{\th{Workload}\th{WAVL}\th{IntMap}\th{IntMap / WAVL}}
\tr{\td{one set as the union of 20,000 stretches}\td{24.8}\td{28.3}\td{1.14}}
\tr{\td{union of two sets of 1,000 stretches, 200 times}\td{96.4}\td{112.8}\td{1.17}}
\tr{\td{intersection of the same, 200 times}\td{90.7}\td{131.0}\td{1.45}}
\tr{\td{difference of the same, 200 times}\td{96.0}\td{124.5}\td{1.30}}
\tr{\td{complement, 200 times}\td{41.9}\td{54.4}\td{1.30}}
\tr{\td{5,000,000 lookups in a function of 2,000 stretches}\td{273.6}\td{300.1}\td{1.10}}
\tr{\td{a wide stretch united with a set of 2,000 stretches, 2,000 times}\td{144.3}\td{154.5}\td{1.07}}
\tr{\td{two unions and a comparison of sets of 2,000 stretches, 1,000 times}\td{114.9}\td{28.4}\td{0.25}}
}

\p{Building sets, the set operations and lookups are 10 to 45 percent slower on \code{\verb~|IntMap~}: they are made of nearest-key lookups, which the balanced tree answers faster. \code{\verb~|IntMap~} is faster at comparing two maps. The WAVL map stays.}

\p{\strong{The translation.} UTF-8 keeps order: a larger code point has a larger encoding, compared byte by byte, and among encodings of one length the bytes after the first each run over \code{\verb~|80~} to \code{\verb~|BF~}. So a stretch of code points is a stretch of byte strings, and it is cut into pieces that are each a sequence of byte ranges:}

\ol{
\li{Cut the stretch where the length of the encoding changes: after U+007F, U+07FF and U+FFFF. The surrogates are already outside every set.}
\li{In a piece of one length, look at the first byte in which the two ends differ. If the bytes after it do not run from all \code{\verb~|80~} to all \code{\verb~|BF~}, cut the piece so that they do: once after the lower end's last code point with that prefix, once before the upper end's first.}
\li{A piece that needs no more cuts is one sequence: for each byte position, the range from the lower end's byte to the upper end's byte.}
}

\p{Worked example, \code{\verb~|[α-ω]~}, U+03B1 to U+03C9. The ends encode as \code{\verb~|CE B1~} and \code{\verb~|CF 89~}. They differ in the first byte, and the second bytes do not run from \code{\verb~|80~} to \code{\verb~|BF~}, so the stretch is cut after U+03BF, the last code point that starts with \code{\verb~|CE~}:}

\pre{\verb~|
U+03B1 – U+03BF      CE [B1-BF]
U+03C0 – U+03C9      CF [80-89]

[α-ω]  =  CE [B1-BF]  |  CF [80-89]
~}

\p{The set of all characters comes out as the nine sequences of well-formed UTF-8:}

\table{
\tr{\th{Code points}\th{Sequence}}
\tr{\td{U+0000 – U+007F}\td{\code{\verb~|[00-7F]~}}}
\tr{\td{U+0080 – U+07FF}\td{\code{\verb~|[C2-DF] [80-BF]~}}}
\tr{\td{U+0800 – U+0FFF}\td{\code{\verb~|E0 [A0-BF] [80-BF]~}}}
\tr{\td{U+1000 – U+CFFF}\td{\code{\verb~|[E1-EC] [80-BF] [80-BF]~}}}
\tr{\td{U+D000 – U+D7FF}\td{\code{\verb~|ED [80-9F] [80-BF]~}}}
\tr{\td{U+E000 – U+FFFF}\td{\code{\verb~|[EE-EF] [80-BF] [80-BF]~}}}
\tr{\td{U+10000 – U+3FFFF}\td{\code{\verb~|F0 [90-BF] [80-BF] [80-BF]~}}}
\tr{\td{U+40000 – U+FFFFF}\td{\code{\verb~|[F1-F3] [80-BF] [80-BF] [80-BF]~}}}
\tr{\td{U+100000 – U+10FFFF}\td{\code{\verb~|F4 [80-8F] [80-BF] [80-BF]~}}}
}

\p{A set becomes the union of the sequences of its stretches, a regex over bytes. The stretches come in order, and so do the sequences, so the union is built in one pass.}

\p{\strong{How large sets are kept small.} A set such as the letters of Unicode has hundreds of stretches. Three things keep its automaton small.}

\ul{
\li{The translation is a union of concatenations built through the normalizing constructors (decision 1), grouped by first byte, so two sequences with the same tail share that tail as one node: \code{\verb~|[E1-EC] T~} and \code{\verb~|[EE-EF] T~} have one \code{\verb~|T = [80-BF] [80-BF]~}. The states reached after the first byte are the distinct tails, not the sequences.}
\li{A set is translated once for each conversion, whatever the number of rules that use it, and after the sets inside a union have been merged.}
\li{The classes of bytes cannot exceed 256, and the table merges equal columns afterwards (decision 6).}
}

\p{Measured on the tables of Unicode 16.0:}

\table{
\tr{\th{Set}\th{Stretches of code points}\th{Sequences}\th{States with shared tails}\th{Classes of bytes}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|[α-ω]~}}\td{1}\td{2}\td{3}\td{5}}
\tr{\td{any character}\td{2}\td{9}\td{8}\td{12}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|[^a-z]~}, complement on code points}\td{3}\td{10}\td{8}\td{12}}
\tr{\td{letters, \code{\verb~|\p{L}~}}\td{677}\td{827}\td{298}\td{95}}
\tr{\td{uppercase letters, \code{\verb~|\p{Lu}~}}\td{651}\td{664}\td{74}\td{87}}
\tr{\td{numbers, \code{\verb~|\p{N}~}}\td{144}\td{149}\td{92}\td{77}}
\tr{\td{word characters: letters, numbers, marks, connectors}\td{825}\td{1,033}\td{328}\td{96}}
}

\p{"States with shared tails" is the number of states an automaton for the set alone needs, the start state included. The count of classes is for that set alone.}
}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-text]{
\title{4. What complement, any character and malformed input mean over bytes}

\p{A regex whose leaves are all sets of characters is called a text regex here. The translation of a set matches exactly the encodings of its members, and concatenation, union, star and intersection of regexes that match only whole characters match only whole characters. So a text regex without complement matches whole characters only, and a match that starts where a character starts ends where one ends.}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Complement of a set}, such as \code{\verb~|[^a-z]~}, is taken on code points before the translation: all characters without the members. It contains no byte string that is not a character.}
\li{\strong{Any character} is the set of all characters, the nine sequences above. It is not "any byte".}
\li{\strong{Complement of a regex.} Taken over all byte strings, the complement of \code{\verb~|a~} would contain the single byte \code{\verb~|CE~}, half of a character. The complement of a text regex is therefore taken within well-formed text: \code{\verb~|not(r)~} is \code{\verb~|U* & ¬r~}, where \code{\verb~|U~} is any character. The result is a text regex again. The complement of a regex that has a set of bytes among its leaves is taken over all byte strings.}
\li{\strong{Malformed UTF-8} needs no rule of its own. No text regex has a transition for a byte that cannot continue an encoding, so the automaton reaches the dead state there, exactly as for a well-formed character that no rule accepts. The match is the longest one before that byte. A matcher that should accept such bytes says so with a rule over sets of bytes.}
\li{\strong{The middle of a character} is a state like any other, as state 2 in the example. It never accepts for a text regex.}
}
}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-actions]{
\title{5. A state is a vector of regexes; a match names rules, and actions are looked up by rule}

\p{As in Paguroidea, a state holds one node per rule, and a rule accepts in a state when its node accepts the empty string.}

\p{What the automaton produces is rule indices, nothing else:}

\pre{\verb~|
pub struct Match { start: u64, length: u64, rule: u32, accept: u32 }
~}

\ul{
\li{\code{\verb~|rule~} is the winner: the earliest rule that accepts at the longest length.}
\li{\code{\verb~|accept~} identifies the set of all rules that accept there. \code{\verb~|lexer.rules(m)~} lists them in rule order. Two rules such as a keyword and an identifier both match \code{\verb~|while~}; the winner is the keyword, and the list has both.}
}

\p{Actions sit beside the automaton, in a table indexed by rule. The matcher is generic in their type \code{\verb~|T~} and offers three ways to use them, all built on \code{\verb~|Match~}:}

\table{
\tr{\th{Call}\th{Gives}\th{Use}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|lexer.next(source)~}}\td{the next \code{\verb~|Match~}}\td{the caller decides what to do with it}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|lexer.action(m)~}}\td{the value of type \code{\verb~|T~} stored for \code{\verb~|m.rule~}}\td{\code{\verb~|T~} is data, such as a token kind: the caller dispatches with \code{\verb~|match~}}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|lexer.fold(source, state)~}}\td{the final state}\td{\code{\verb~|T~} is a function from a state and a view of the matched text to a new state: the matcher calls the action of every match in turn}}
}

\p{\code{\verb~|fold~} is the dispatch form: each rule's function is called as its rule matches, and the state it threads can collect every match.}

\p{An action is given the matched text as a \code{\verb~|TextView~} of the standard library: a range of bytes together with the \code{\verb~|Text~} it lies in. The \code{\verb~|Text~} says who owns the bytes: \code{\verb~|Literal~} for a string literal, which nothing needs to keep alive, or \code{\verb~|Owned~} for a string, which the view shares. So a view stays valid for as long as the action or its state keeps it, whatever the matcher does next, and the action can tell which case it has from \code{\verb~|view.base()~}. The pointer and length the matcher itself reads through are never given to an action. A list of all matches is a fold that appends; \code{\verb~|lexer.matches(source)~} is that, for any \code{\verb~|T~}.}

\p{A rule may be marked as skipped, for white space and comments: \code{\verb~|next~} goes on to the following match, and \code{\verb~|fold~} does not call an action for it.}

\p{Why the automaton holds indices and not the values of \code{\verb~|T~}: states are compared and hashed while the table is built, which a \code{\verb~|T~} need not support; a set of accepting rules is a set of small integers; and a completed table does not depend on \code{\verb~|T~}.}

\p{A single regex is the case of one rule.}
}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-table]{
\title{6. The table is built when rules become a matcher}

\p{Turning the rules into a matcher is the one place where everything is known, so the whole table is built there. Nothing is computed while matching, and a matcher is a value that never changes.}

\p{The tables are flat memory that a match reads through pointers, and they depend on all states together, which is why they cannot be filled one entry at a time:}

\pre{\verb~|
classes[byte]              -> column           256 bytes
next[state + column]       -> cell             the number of a state is where its row begins
first[byte]                -> cell             the row of the start state, a cell for every byte
winners[state]             -> the earliest rule that accepts
run_words[state], vectors  -> the run of a state (decision 7)
lookup                     -> the lookup table that the runs share (decision 7)
~}

\p{A row is as long as the number of columns rounded up to a power of two, eight at least. That leaves the three lowest bits of a state number free, and a cell holds in them what reaching its state takes:}

\table{
\tr{\th{Kind}\th{The state}\th{What the match does}}
\tr{\td{0}\td{the dead state}\td{ends; the match is the last one found}}
\tr{\td{1}\td{accepts, and no byte goes on}\td{ends with this state}}
\tr{\td{2}\td{accepts}\td{takes it as the match so far, then steps}}
\tr{\td{3}\td{accepts and has a run, and no other byte goes on}\td{reads the run and ends}}
\tr{\td{4}\td{accepts and has a run}\td{reads the run, takes it as the match so far, then steps}}
\tr{\td{5}\td{has a run}\td{reads the run, then steps}}
\tr{\td{6}\td{neither}\td{steps}}
}

\p{The states are numbered in this order, so the states that accept are one stretch of numbers and the states with a run another, and \code{\verb~|winners~} and the runs are indexed without gaps.}

\p{The steps, in order:}

\ol{
\li{\strong{Bytes.} Merge the sets inside each union and intersection, then translate every set of characters to its byte sequences (decision 3). From here on every leaf is a set of bytes.}
\li{\strong{Classes.} Fold \code{\verb~|refine~} over every set of bytes in the rules (decision 2).}
\li{\strong{Nodes.} Turn the regex trees into nodes through the constructors (decision 1). A set node holds the classes of its set.}
\li{\strong{States.} Starting from the vector of the rules' nodes, take the derivative by every class and collect the vectors reached, until no new one appears. This stops with an error above a limit on the number of states, since some regexes have exponentially many.}
\li{\strong{Dead states.} A state is dead when no accepting state can be reached from it. With every transition known, this is one walk backwards from the accepting states. All dead states become the single dead state.}
\li{\strong{Columns.} Two classes whose columns are equal in every state are one column. The classes of step 2 come from the sets as written and may be finer than the automaton needs.}
\li{\strong{Kinds and numbers.} Every live state gets its kind, and the states are numbered kind after kind.}
\li{\strong{Runs.} For every state, the classes on which it loops to itself, and from them its run (decision 7).}
}

\p{Step 5 is what makes intersection and complement cost nothing extra. With them a state can match nothing without being the node \code{\verb~|∅~}: \code{\verb~|[a-z]+ & [0-9]+~} is such a regex. Asking that of a regex needs an emptiness test; asking it of the finished automaton is the walk of step 5, which is needed in any case to stop a match as early as possible.}

\p{A matcher is passed around freely and shared, because it is never updated:}

\pre{\verb~|
let (source, found) = lexer.next(source);
~}
}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-simd]{
\title{7. Acceleration is a skip over bytes}

\p{When the table is built, the matcher computes for every state the bytes on which the state loops to itself, and from them a run. In the loop it skips the run for a state that has one. A state without a run is matched one byte at a time, and nothing else changes.}

\p{The choice of a run is the one Paguroidea's lexer makes. The bytes on which the state loops cost one for a single byte and two for a longer stretch:}

\table{
\tr{\th{Loop set}\th{How the run is skipped}}
\tr{\td{costs at most four}\td{sixteen bytes at a time, compared with the vectors of the set, while every byte is in it}}
\tr{\td{its complement costs at most four}\td{sixteen bytes at a time, compared with the vectors of the complement, while no byte is in it}}
\tr{\td{neither}\td{through the lookup table, eight bytes at a time and then byte by byte}}
}

\p{\strong{Vectors.} Paguroidea generates the comparisons of each state; here their shape is one of eight forms and their constants are data. A single byte is one vector, a stretch is two (its first byte, and its length less one, compared after a subtraction), so a form compares with exactly as many vectors as its set costs: one to four bytes; one stretch; one stretch and one or two bytes; two stretches. Every state with a run has four vectors of sixteen bytes at a place computed from its number, so they are read without first reading anything else about the run.}

\p{\strong{The lookup table.} A set that fits neither way is given a number. Set number \code{\verb~|k~} is bit \code{\verb~|k % 8~} of table \code{\verb~|k / 8~}, a table is 256 bytes, and the bit is set in the bytes that leave the state:}

\pre{\verb~|
lookup[table * 256 + byte] & bit != 0      the byte leaves the state
~}

\p{Equal sets get one number, whichever states they come from, so eight different sets cost 256 bytes together and a state holds only the place of its table and its bit.}

\p{A membership test for an arbitrary set of bytes on a whole vector at once would need a shuffle whose indices are a run-time vector, which \code{\verb~|core::simd~} does not have; it is not part of this design.}

\p{\strong{What it costs to read tables instead of running generated code.} Measured against a lexer generated by Paguroidea's own \code{\verb~|pag-lexer~} from the token rules of its JSON benchmark, on its \code{\verb~|twitter.json~} (767 kB, 84,083 tokens), both built for baseline x86-64:}

\table{
\tr{\th{Tokenizer}\th{MB/s}\th{Instructions per token}}
\tr{\td{Paguroidea, generated code}\td{3,270}\td{69}}
\tr{\td{this matcher, token loop inlined with it}\td{1,990}\td{91}}
\tr{\td{this matcher, one \code{\verb~|unsafe_longest~} call per token}\td{1,620}\td{about 175}}
\tr{\td{this matcher, \code{\verb~|longest~} per token}\td{710}\td{}}
\tr{\td{this matcher, \code{\verb~|fold~} with a closure}\td{660}\td{about 260}}
}

\p{By kind of token, in nanoseconds per token with the loop inlined: a one-byte token 0.97 against 0.93; a string of 400 bytes 10.2 against 11.0; a short run (four blanks, a short string, a number) about 5.7 against 3.4. The skip loops are as fast as the generated ones, and a token without a run costs the same. A short run is slower because the vectors are read from memory at a place that depends on the state, which is itself read from a table, where generated code has them as constants.}

\p{Three choices came from measuring. The kind of a state is carried in its cell and jumped on, because comparing the state number against bounds compiled to conditional moves, and the next position then waited for every table read: 2.9 times slower than Paguroidea became 1.6. The start state has a row with a cell for every byte, which makes the first step of a match one read instead of two. Vectors are stored ready and by state number, because building them from bytes costs four instructions each on baseline x86-64 and finding them through the run word costs one more read.}

\p{The two lower rows of the table are not the matcher: a closure that is called in a loop is copied for every call, and \code{\verb~|Option~} is allocated.}

\p{For a text regex, a state that repeats a set of characters loops to itself on the ASCII members of the set only. A character beyond ASCII leaves the state and comes back to it one to three table steps later. So the skip runs through ASCII, and other characters are stepped through the table:}

\table{
\tr{\th{Repeated set}\th{Bytes the state loops on}\th{Effect}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|[a-zα-ω]~}}\td{\code{\verb~|[61-7A]~}}\td{the skip runs through ASCII letters; a Greek letter takes two table steps, after which the skip resumes}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|[^"\\]~}, the inside of a string literal}\td{ASCII without the quote and the backslash}\td{the skip runs through ASCII text; each character beyond ASCII is stepped through the table}}
}
}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-home]{
\title{8. Where it lives}

\p{A module \code{\verb~|std::regex~}, with nothing added to the compiler.}
}
}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-input]{
\title{Reading input}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-window]{
\title{The window}

\p{Inside one scan the matcher reads input through a window: a pointer to the first byte and a number of bytes.}

\pre{\verb~|
pub struct Window { base: ConstPtr<Generic>, len: u64 }
~}

\p{A window holds a raw pointer, so it never leaves \code{\verb~|std::regex~}:}

\ul{
\li{\code{\verb~|Window~} is private to the module. No public function takes or returns one.}
\li{The matcher makes a window from a \code{\verb~|TextView~} at the start of a scan and holds that view until the scan returns, so the pointer cannot outlive the bytes.}
\li{The functions that take a window, the step and the skip, are private to the module.}
\li{What a caller sees is positions as numbers in a \code{\verb~|Match~} and text as a \code{\verb~|TextView~}.}
}

\p{The window is what makes looking ahead possible. A step reads the byte at a position; a skip loads a vector of bytes starting at a position. Neither reads at or past \code{\verb~|len~}: the last chunk is loaded under the mask \code{\verb~|mask_first(len - at)~}, so no byte outside the window is touched and there is no scalar loop for the remainder.}
}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-loop]{
\title{The matching loop}

\p{The position of a match in progress is three values:}

\pre{\verb~|
pub struct Scan { state: u32, at: u64, accepted: Option<Accept> }   // Accept: the rule and the length at which it accepted
~}

\p{One call advances a scan over the bytes a source holds:}

\pre{\verb~|
fn run(lexer, scan, held: TextView) -> (scan, Status)      // Status: Dead or Exhausted
window = the pointer and length of `held`

loop {
    match cell & 7 {
        0 => return Dead                                    // the match is `accepted`
        1 => accepted = (cell, at) ; return Dead
        6 => step
        _ => if kind >= 3 { at = skip(cell, window, at) }
             if kind != 5 { accepted = (cell, at) }
             if kind == 3 { return Dead } else { step }
    }
}

step:
    if at == window.len             { return Exhausted }
    cell = next[(cell & !7) + classes[byte at `at`]] ; at = at + 1
~}

\p{\code{\verb~|Dead~} means no rule can match further: the result is \code{\verb~|accepted~}. \code{\verb~|Exhausted~} means the window ended while some rule could still match.}
}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-skip]{
\title{The skip}

\p{For a state that loops on the bytes \code{\verb~|[a-z]~}, the skip is this function. \code{\verb~|first~} and \code{\verb~|span~} are two of the vectors of the state: sixteen times \code{\verb~|a~}, and sixteen times 25.}

\pre{\verb~|
fn stretched(vectors: ConstPtr<Generic>, inside: bool, bytes: ConstPtr<Generic>, at: u64, length: u64) -> u64 {
    let first = chunk_at(vectors, 0);
    let span = chunk_at(vectors, 16);
    let (reached, _) = while [at = at, going = true] (going && at + 16 <= length) {
        let hit = simd::le(simd::sub(chunk_at(bytes, at), first), span);
        let leaving = if inside { simd::not(hit) } else { hit };
        if simd::any(leaving) { (at + simd::first_set(leaving), false) } else { (at + 16, true) }
    };
    reached
}
~}

\p{The loop carries only the position. Fewer than sixteen bytes at the end of the input are read one at a time through the table, so the skip always stops at the first byte that leaves the state or at the end. A match that ends after its run (kind 3) relies on that.}
}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-source]{
\title{Input that arrives in pieces}

\p{A source hands the matcher the bytes it holds, as a \code{\verb~|TextView~}, and can be asked for more. It is a public trait and has no pointer in it:}

\pre{\verb~|
pub trait Source {
    /// The bytes held from the start of the current token on, as a view that
    /// shares their owner.
    fn held(self: Self) -> TextView;
    /// Read more bytes after those held, keeping them. False at the end of input.
    fn extend(self: Self) -> (Self, bool);
    /// Drop the first `count` bytes: a token was returned.
    fn consume(self: Self, count: u64) -> Self;
}
~}

\p{A \code{\verb~|Text~}, literal or owned, is a source that holds all of it and never extends. The text of a match is the first \code{\verb~|length~} bytes of \code{\verb~|held()~}, a range of that \code{\verb~|Text~}. A buffered reader keeps the bytes of the current token in a string and appends to them; its views share that string.}

\p{A view of an owned string shares it. If an action keeps such a view and the reader then needs to append, the string is no longer held by the reader alone, and the reader continues in a new string with the bytes not yet consumed. An action that only reads the view and lets it go costs nothing. This cost is accepted: it is what sharing means, and it is paid only by an action that keeps a view.}

\p{\code{\verb~|next~} is then:}

\pre{\verb~|
scan = start
loop {
    (scan, status) = run(lexer, scan, source.held())    // a window is made from the view inside `run`
    if status is Dead                    { break }
    (source, more) = source.extend()     // Exhausted: the window ended
    if !more                             { break }
}
return the action and length of scan.accepted, and source.consume(length)
~}

\p{The scan is kept across \code{\verb~|extend~}, so the matcher never reads a byte twice, and a skip that stopped at the end of one piece goes on in the next. The bytes of the token stay in the window until \code{\verb~|consume~}, because the longest match may end before the last byte read.}

\p{A piece may end inside a character. The state of the scan then is a state in the middle of that character, and the next piece continues from it; no bytes are set aside and nothing is read again.}

\p{For a caller that pushes pieces instead of being asked for them, the library provides a source that owns a buffer:}

\pre{\verb~|
let stream = Stream::new(lexer);
let (stream, matches) = stream.feed(piece);      // every match that is certain so far
let (stream, matches) = stream.finish();         // the end of input
~}

\p{\code{\verb~|Stream~} holds the scan and the bytes not yet consumed, beside the lexer. A match is certain once the scan is \code{\verb~|Dead~}; until then the bytes of the token in progress are kept.}
}
}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-data]{
\title{Data}

\p{The tree a caller builds, \code{\verb~|Regex~}. Its translation, \code{\verb~|ByteRegex~}, is the same without the set of characters, so the stages after it have no case for one:}

\table{
\tr{\th{Leaf or operator}\th{Holds}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|∅~}, \code{\verb~|ε~}}\td{nothing}}
\tr{\td{set of bytes}\td{a \code{\verb~|Bits~}}}
\tr{\td{set of characters}\td{a \code{\verb~|CharSet~}}}
\tr{\td{concatenation, union, intersection}\td{two trees}}
\tr{\td{star, complement}\td{one tree}}
}

\p{Nodes, in a table that makes equal nodes the same integer:}

\table{
\tr{\th{Node}\th{Children}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|∅~}}\td{none}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|ε~}}\td{none}}
\tr{\td{set}\td{a set of classes, as a bit per class}}
\tr{\td{concatenation}\td{two nodes}}
\tr{\td{star}\td{one node}}
\tr{\td{union, intersection}\td{a sorted list of nodes without duplicates}}
\tr{\td{complement}\td{one node}}
}

\p{A set node holds classes, not bytes: once the classes of a matcher are computed, every set is a union of classes, and the derivative of a set by a class is one bit test. There is no node for a set of characters.}

\p{Tables of a matcher:}

\table{
\tr{\th{Table}\th{Indexed by}\th{Entry}}
\tr{\td{class map}\td{byte, 256 entries}\td{class}}
\tr{\td{columns}\td{class}\td{column of the transition table}}
\tr{\td{rows}\td{state}\td{row of the transition table}}
\tr{\td{transitions}\td{row × column}\td{state, with one reserved value: dead}}
\tr{\td{accepting}\td{state}\td{none, or the winning rule and the set of accepting rules}}
\tr{\td{accepting sets}\td{set id}\td{the rules that accept, in rule order}}
\tr{\td{runs}\td{state}\td{none, or the run for its loop set}}
\tr{\td{states}\td{state}\td{its vector of nodes; and the reverse map from a vector to its state}}
\tr{\td{rules}\td{rule index}\td{the action of type \code{\verb~|T~}, and whether the rule is skipped}}
}
}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-interface]{
\title{Interface sketch}

\p{Every value here is immutable; each call returns a new value. No collection is part of the interface: rules are added one at a time, and matches are consumed by a fold.}

\pre{\verb~|
import std::regex::{Regex, Rules, Lexer};
import std::regex::alphabet::char_set::CharSet;

let letter = Regex::chars(CharSet::range('a', 'z').union(CharSet::range('α', 'ω')));
let digit = Regex::range(48, 57);
let space = Regex::byte(32);

// Data as actions: the caller dispatches.
let lexer = Rules::new()
    .rule(letter.plus(), Token::Ident)
    .rule(digit.plus(), Token::Number)
    .skip(space.plus())
    .lexer();

let (source, found) = lexer.next(source);       // Some(Match { start: 0, length: 3, rule: 0, .. })
match found {
    Some(m) => match lexer.action(m) { Token::Ident => ..., Token::Number => ... },
    None => ...
}

// Functions as actions: the matcher dispatches, over all matches.
let counter = Rules::new()
    .rule(letter.plus(), |words: u64, text: TextView| words + 1)
    .skip(space.plus())
    .lexer();
let words = counter.fold(source, 0);
~}

\table{
\tr{\th{Value}\th{Made by}\th{Used by}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|Regex~}}\td{\code{\verb~|empty~}, \code{\verb~|epsilon~}, \code{\verb~|bytes~}, \code{\verb~|byte~}, \code{\verb~|range~}, \code{\verb~|chars~}, \code{\verb~|char~}, \code{\verb~|any_char~}, \code{\verb~|literal~}; and from regexes: \code{\verb~|then~}, \code{\verb~|or~}, \code{\verb~|and~}, \code{\verb~|not~}, \code{\verb~|star~}, \code{\verb~|plus~}, \code{\verb~|optional~}}\td{\code{\verb~|rule~}, \code{\verb~|skip~}}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|Rules<T>~}}\td{\code{\verb~|Rules::new()~}, then \code{\verb~|rule(regex, action)~} and \code{\verb~|skip(regex)~}}\td{\code{\verb~|lexer()~}}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|Lexer<T>~}}\td{\code{\verb~|rules.lexer()~}, which builds the table and fails above the state limit}\td{\code{\verb~|next~}, \code{\verb~|action~}, \code{\verb~|rules~}, \code{\verb~|fold~}, \code{\verb~|fold_matches~}, \code{\verb~|matches~}}}
}

\p{\code{\verb~|fold~} is the general way to consume matches: \code{\verb~|lexer.fold(source, state)~} with functions as actions, each from a state and a \code{\verb~|TextView~} to a state, or \code{\verb~|lexer.fold_matches(source, state, step)~} with a function \code{\verb~|step~} from a state, a \code{\verb~|Match~} and a \code{\verb~|TextView~} for any action type. \code{\verb~|matches~} is \code{\verb~|fold_matches~} that builds a list.}
}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-open]{
\title{Open questions}

\ul{
\li{Whether one regex may mix sets of bytes and sets of characters. Decision 4 allows it and takes the complement of such a regex over all byte strings; forbidding the mix would make every regex either text or bytes.}
\li{Whether a state that repeats a set containing every character beyond ASCII, such as \code{\verb~|[^"\\]~}, should skip the bytes \code{\verb~|80~} to \code{\verb~|FF~} too. It would run through text in any script at vector speed, and it would also run through malformed bytes that the table would stop at.}
\li{Whether "all matches" should also mean a search for one regex anywhere in the input. That needs an automaton for \code{\verb~|.*r~} and is left to a later version here.}
\li{Whether the translation of a set is kept from one conversion to the next. Here it is done again for each matcher.}
}
}
